TITLE:: SPTree
summary:: the server's node tree, its busses, what looks wrong, where a signal goes, what changed
categories:: Libraries>ServerPanel
related:: Classes/SPInspector, Classes/SPRouting, Classes/SPProbe, Classes/SPSnapshot, Classes/SPLog, Classes/ServerPanel, Classes/Group

DESCRIPTION::
What code::s.plotTree:: shows, made legible, and a place to debug from. One row per node, indented by depth, with guides down the side.

list::
## A strong::group:: says what it is when that can be known: the root, the default group, an code::Ndef::, SuperDirt and each of its orbits, a ParaEQ fx chain. Its row says how many synths are inside, folded or not. Click its triangle (or double click the row) to fold it away; strong::open all:: unfolds everything.
## A strong::synth:: shows a light (green when the busses it writes carry signal, red for a NaN or an inf), its SynthDef with a dot in a colour of its own (the same def is always the same colour), and at the right what it is, from link::#*describers::. A paused synth says so; a red dot at the right says something looks wrong with it. With strong::controls:: on, the row carries its controls and their values.
## A click on a row shows what it is about in the strong::inspector:: at the right (link::Classes/SPInspector::): a node, a bus or a buffer. Up and down move the selection.
## A node that has just appeared is lit in orange for a second.
## The filter keeps the rows that contain what you type.
## The strong::hint bar:: along the bottom says what the pointer is over - every control, row and step has a hint - and, over nothing, how to find your way in the view that is shown. The strong::hints:: box at the top right folds it away (link::#*showHints::).
::

The repository's code::docs/node-tree-tutorial.md:: walks through the window from the first click.

subsection:: The views

The menu at the top left (link::#-mode::) changes what the list shows.

table::
## strong::tree:: || the nodes, in the order the server runs them
## strong::busses:: || every bus in use: who writes it, who reads it, its level. A double click follows its signal
## strong::problems:: || what looks wrong: a synth that reads a bus before it is written (it hears silence), synths of a def that pile up, a synth that outlives its kind, a paused synth beside its running twin, a NaN on a bus, a FAILURE of the server. The button of that name is red, with a count, while there are any
## strong::signal path:: || where the selected synth's sound goes, bus by bus with levels, to the outputs - and where it stops (link::#-showPath::)
## strong::log:: || every node that started, ended, was paused or resumed, with the time (link::Classes/SPLog::)
## strong::ended:: || the synths that have ended, newest first; click one to see what it was
## strong::buffers:: || the buffers this sclang made, and which synths read them
## strong::changes:: || what is different since a snapshot (link::#-compare::)
::

The rules behind the problems, and what each view can not know, are in the repository's code::docs/node-tree.md::.

subsection:: Readable while things change fast

With link::#-calm:: on (the default) the list is laid out - rows added, removed, moved - at most once every link::#*calmTime:: seconds, whatever the server does; what is at the top of the view stays there; a group that makes link::#*busyRate:: or more nodes a second shows its short-lived children as one line (click it to have them one by one); a node that ended keeps its row, dimmed, until the next layout; and numbers are written again twice a second at most. A synth that lives a fraction of a second is still caught: a new group's contents are asked for the moment it appears (code::/n_go::), and a new synth's group is asked for, so both are in the log and under strong::ended::.

subsection:: Stepping back

The strip under the top row is the last link::#*rewind:: seconds: nodes that started (up) and ended (down) each half second, an orange tick for a problem, a red one for a FAILURE, a white line for a snapshot. Every answer of the server is kept that long. Hold the window still (space) and click the strip to see that moment; left and right step one answer; strong::run:: goes back to now.

strong::snapshots:: keeps the whole tree at one moment (link::#-snapshot::), shows one again, compares, and writes one to disk (link::Classes/SPSnapshot::).

It asks the server with code::/g_queryTree:: every half second (strong::every:: changes that). One per server; ServerPanel's code::p:: key opens it, and so does code::s.plotTree::.

code::
s.boot;
SPTree(s);
SPTree(s).mode = \busses;
SPTree(s).filter = "dirt";
SPTree(s).inspect(s.defaultGroup.nodeID);
SPTree(s).snapshot;
::

subsection:: Names of your own

A group's name comes from the functions in link::#*labellers::, what a node is from link::#*describers:: (and, last, link::#*notes::), what a bus is from link::#*busNamers::. All are lists you can add to; SuperDirtGUI adds its own describer, bus names and labels this way while it runs.

code::
~g = Group(s);
SPTree.labellers.add({ |server, labels| labels[~g.nodeID] = "my group" });
SPTree.notes.addFirst(["^my_", "one of mine"]);
SPTree.describers.addFirst({ |server, node|
	if (node[\path].includes(~g.nodeID)) { (name: "one of mine", where: "my group", text: "made by my pattern") }
});
SPTree.busNamers.addFirst({ |server, rate, index| if (rate == \audio and: { index == 20 }) { "my send" } });
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
The node tree of a server, opened if it is not.
ARGUMENT:: server
A link::Classes/Server::; code::Server.default:: when nil.
RETURNS:: an SPTree

METHOD:: labellers
A link::Classes/List:: of functions code::{ |server, labels| }::, each putting nodeID -> name into code::labels:: (an link::Classes/IdentityDictionary::). Run before every redraw of the outline; an error in one is ignored.

METHOD:: notes
A link::Classes/List:: of code::[regexp, text]:: pairs: a synth whose def matches the regexp is described by the text. The first that matches wins.

METHOD:: describers
A link::Classes/List:: of functions code::{ |server, node| }:: saying what a node is. code::node:: is an link::Classes/Event:: with code::id::, code::kind:: (code::\synth:: or code::\group::), code::def::, code::parent::, code::path:: (the ids of the groups above it, root first) and, when known, code::controls:: (code::[name, value]:: pairs). A describer answers nil or an Event with any of: code::name:: (a few words, also shown at the right of the row), code::where::, code::text::, code::file:: (a source file, for the inspector's strong::source:: button), code::actions:: (code::[[label, { |node, server| }] ...]::, buttons in the inspector). Every describer is asked; for each key the first answer wins, actions add up. Use code::addFirst:: to speak before the ones built in (an Ndef's synth, SuperDirt's orbit effects and event synths, the notes). An error in one is ignored.

METHOD:: describe
What link::#*describers:: say about a node.
ARGUMENT:: server
The link::Classes/Server::.
ARGUMENT:: node
An Event as above.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::, empty when nothing is known

METHOD:: busNamers
A link::Classes/List:: of functions code::{ |server, rate, index| }:: answering a bus's name or nil; the first answer wins. Built in: BusManager's names, SuperDirt's orbit busses (code::orbit 0 dry::), the hardware.

METHOD:: busName
A bus in words, from link::#*busNamers::.
ARGUMENT:: server
The link::Classes/Server::.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio:: or code::\control::.
ARGUMENT:: index
The bus index.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::, or nil

METHOD:: checkers
A link::Classes/List:: of functions code::{ |server, model| }:: answering an Array of problems only a system of yours knows to look for. code::model:: is link::Classes/SPRouting::'s. A problem is an Event: code::level:: (code::\warn:: or code::\info::), code::text::, code::key:: (a String that stays the same while the problem does), and code::id:: (a node) or code::bus:: (code::[rate, index]::) to say what it is about. An error in one is ignored.

METHOD:: showHints
Whether the hint bar shows, in every window of the family (the scopes and the meters have one too). The strong::hints:: box sets it.

METHOD:: modes
The views, as Symbols: code::\tree \busses \problems \path \log \recent \buffers \diff::.

METHOD:: modeNames
What the menu calls them.

METHOD:: calmTime
With link::#-calm:: on, the least time between two layouts of the list, in seconds: 0.7.

METHOD:: busyRate
How many new nodes a second in one group make it show them as one line: 2.

METHOD:: rewind
How many seconds of the server's answers are kept to step back through: 90.

METHOD:: busKey
The key of a bus's row.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio:: or code::\control::.
ARGUMENT:: index
The bus index.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/Symbol::

METHOD:: dbText
A level in words: code::"-14 dB"::, code::"silent"::.
ARGUMENT:: amp
An amplitude.

METHOD:: dirts
A link::Classes/List:: of SuperDirt instances the tree should know. code::SuperDirt.start:: sets no code::SuperDirt.default::, so beside that and code::~dirt:: the tree looks here; SuperDirtGUI puts its own in.

METHOD:: historySize
How many ended synths link::#-history:: keeps, 300 by default.

METHOD:: defColor
The colour of a SynthDef's dot: the same def, the same colour.
ARGUMENT:: def
A SynthDef's name.

METHOD:: noteOf
What link::#*notes:: say a def is.
ARGUMENT:: def
A SynthDef's name.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::, or nil

METHOD:: intervals
The intervals the window offers, in seconds.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: interval
How often the server is asked, in seconds.

METHOD:: showControls
Whether each synth's controls are asked for and shown.

METHOD:: filter
Only the synths whose def contains this, and the groups they are in; an empty string shows everything.

METHOD:: mode
What the list shows, one of link::#*modes::.

METHOD:: defaultHint
What the hint bar says while the pointer is over nothing in particular: how to find your way in the view that is shown, and, held still, how to step back.

METHOD:: calm
Whether the list is kept readable while the server changes fast (see above). True by default.

METHOD:: running
Whether it follows the server. Held still, it shows the last answer, or the moment chosen in the strip; setting it true goes back to now.

METHOD:: selectBus
Show a bus in the inspector: its level, who writes it and who reads it.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio:: or code::\control::.
ARGUMENT:: index
A bus index; a channel of a stereo pair selects the pair.
ARGUMENT:: channels
How many, when the bus is in no row.

METHOD:: selBus
The selected bus as code::[rate, index, channels]::, or nil.

METHOD:: selectBuffer
Show a buffer in the inspector.
ARGUMENT:: bufnum
The buffer's number.

METHOD:: selBuffer
The selected buffer's number, or nil.

METHOD:: showPath
The signal path view, from a synth or a bus; with nothing given, from what is selected.
ARGUMENT:: what
A synth's node id, or a bus as code::[rate, index, channels]::.

METHOD:: pathRoot
What the path starts from: code::(kind: \synth, id:):: or code::(kind: \bus, rate:, index:, channels:)::.

METHOD:: model
The tree as busses, from link::Classes/SPRouting#*build::, made again at every answer of the server.

METHOD:: problems
What looks wrong now: an Array of Events (code::level::, code::kind::, code::id::, code::bus::, code::text::, code::key::).

METHOD:: problemsOf
The problems about one node.
ARGUMENT:: id
A node id.

METHOD:: problemsOfBus
The problems about a bus.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio:: or code::\control::.
ARGUMENT:: index
The first channel.
ARGUMENT:: channels
How many.

METHOD:: levelOf
A bus channel's peak now (or at the moment looked at); nil when it is not measured.
ARGUMENT:: index
An audio bus channel.

METHOD:: levelOver
The highest of link::#-levelOf:: over a run of channels.
ARGUMENT:: index
The first channel.
ARGUMENT:: channels
How many.

METHOD:: outLevel
What a synth puts out: the highest level on the busses it writes. Nil when they are not known.
ARGUMENT:: id
A synth's node id.

METHOD:: isBad
Whether a bus the synth writes carries a NaN, an inf or denormals.
ARGUMENT:: id
A synth's node id.

METHOD:: iosOf
A synth's busses, as link::Classes/SPRouting#*iosOf:: answers them.
ARGUMENT:: id
A synth's node id.

METHOD:: pausedIds
The ids of the synths known to be paused.

METHOD:: steadyDb
A level as text that holds still long enough to be read: written again twice a second at most.
ARGUMENT:: key
Whose level it is, a Symbol.
ARGUMENT:: amp
The level.

METHOD:: probe
The link::Classes/SPProbe:: that measures the busses.

METHOD:: log
The link::Classes/SPLog::.

METHOD:: health
The link::Classes/SPHealth:: that finds what only shows over time.

METHOD:: watch
The link::Classes/SPWatch:: of watched values.

METHOD:: watchToggle
Keep a value in view under the list, or stop.
ARGUMENT:: what
code::(kind: \control, id:, index:, name:, def:):: or code::(kind: \bus, rate:, index:, name:)::.

METHOD:: isWatched
Whether that value is watched.
ARGUMENT:: what
As for link::#-watchToggle::.

METHOD:: listen
Play an audio bus on the outputs (link::Classes/SPProbe#*listenOut::), beside what plays there; again on the same bus stops it.
ARGUMENT:: bus
The first channel.
ARGUMENT:: channels
One or two.

METHOD:: isListeningTo
Whether that bus is being listened to.
ARGUMENT:: bus
The first channel.

METHOD:: snapshot
Keep the tree as it is now: every node with its controls, the busses and their levels, the problems. While it runs, the snapshot is made from the server's next answer; it is then the first of link::#-snapshots::.

METHOD:: snapshots
The snapshots taken, newest first, twelve at most. Each is an Event (link::Classes/SPSnapshot::).

METHOD:: showSnapshot
Look at a snapshot: the tree as it was, held still.
ARGUMENT:: snap
One of link::#-snapshots::.

METHOD:: compare
The changes view: what is different from one snapshot to another.
ARGUMENT:: from
A snapshot.
ARGUMENT:: to
Another, or nil for the tree as it is now.

METHOD:: diffFrom
The snapshot the changes view starts from.

METHOD:: diffTo
The one it ends at; nil is now.

METHOD:: saveSnapshot
Write a snapshot to link::Classes/SPSnapshot#*folder::, as an archive and as text.
ARGUMENT:: snap
A snapshot; the newest when nil.
RETURNS:: the archive's path

METHOD:: saveLog
Write the log to link::Classes/SPSnapshot#*folder:: as text.
RETURNS:: the file's path

METHOD:: stepFrame
Held still, step through the answers kept.
ARGUMENT:: by
-1 back, 1 forward.

METHOD:: viewing
The moment looked at while held still (an Event with code::time::, code::tree::, code::levels::), or nil.

METHOD:: busRecord
A bus as the inspector shows it.
ARGUMENT:: rate
code::\audio:: or code::\control::.
ARGUMENT:: index
The first channel.
ARGUMENT:: channels
How many.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::

METHOD:: bufferRecord
A buffer as the inspector shows it.
ARGUMENT:: bufnum
The buffer's number.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::

METHOD:: bufferText
A buffer in words: SuperDirt's name, its file, its length, its channels.
ARGUMENT:: bufnum
The buffer's number.

METHOD:: showInspector
Whether the inspector is shown at the right.

METHOD:: captureEvents
Whether a new group's contents are asked for the moment it appears, so the synths of a SuperDirt event are caught. True by default.

METHOD:: select
Show a node in the inspector.
ARGUMENT:: id
A node id: running, or in link::#-history::.

METHOD:: selected
The id of the node shown in the inspector, or nil.

METHOD:: inspect
Bring the window to the front on a node: unfold the groups above it, select it, scroll to it.
ARGUMENT:: id
A node id.

METHOD:: recordOf
Everything known about a node, running or ended: what the tree says, its controls, whether it is paused (code::paused::, code::pausedSince::), when it was first seen (code::born::), when it ended (code::ended::, and code::gone:: true), the groups above it in words (code::pathText::); for a group how many groups and synths it holds and their defs.
ARGUMENT:: id
A node id.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Event::, or nil when nothing is known

METHOD:: history
The synths that have ended since the window opened, newest first: link::#-recordOf::'s records.

METHOD:: running
Whether it follows the server. Paused, it holds the last tree.

METHOD:: front
Bring the window to the front.

METHOD:: close
Close the window.
